The original implementations of the `cleanup` and `closeStream` methods only ignored `IOException`, which meant that any other type of `Throwable` that could be thrown during the cleanup process was not handled. This could lead to unhandled exceptions, resulting in resource leaks or incomplete cleanup operations when non-IO exceptions occurred.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
// Updated implementation of cleanup
// ignore Throwable
```
```java
// Updated implementation of closeStream
// ignore Throwable
```
Reason:
The patch broadens the exception handling in both the `cleanup` and `closeStream` methods to ignore all `Throwable` types instead of just `IOException`. This ensures that any potential exceptions, including non-IO exceptions, are ignored during the cleanup process, preventing resource leaks and ensuring complete cleanup operations.

